<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hans von Bülow</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1894">1894</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hans von Bülow was a German conductor and pianist, one of the first virtuoso conductors. He conducted the premieres of Wagner's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tristan-und-isolde">Tristan und Isolde</a> (1865) and Die Meistersinger (1868), even after his wife Cosima left him for Wagner. As director of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a> court orchestra from 1880 to 1885 he made it a model ensemble, and he later became the first principal conductor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> Philharmonic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hans-von-bulow.jpg" alt="Hans von Bülow" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Kendall Warren · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hans_von_B%C3%BClow,_c1875_(Newberry_Library,_Westervelt_Coll)_front_-_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
